// ─── Glossary: words on the element cards that a 10–14 year old may not know ───
// The first use of each on a card is underlined; pointing at or tapping it shows the meaning.
var GLOSSARY=[
["rare earths?","A family of 17 similar metals. They are not actually rare, just hard to separate from one another."],
["lanthanides?","The row of 15 similar metals from lanthanum to lutetium; most rare earths are lanthanides."],
["actinides?","The row of 15 heavy, radioactive metals from actinium to lawrencium, including uranium."],
["noble gas(?:es)?","Gases such as helium and neon that almost never react with anything."],
["superalloys?","An alloy, usually based on nickel, that stays strong at temperatures where steel would soften."],
["alloy(?:s|ed)?","A metal blended with other elements - usually other metals, sometimes carbon, as in steel - to make it stronger or tougher."],
["superconduct(?:or|ors|ing)","A material that carries electricity with no resistance at all, but only when extremely cold."],
["semiconductors?","A material that conducts electricity only under the right conditions, which is what lets chips switch on and off."],
["cathodes?","The positive side of a battery, where the lithium rests when the battery is flat."],
["anodes?","The negative side of a battery, where the lithium is stored when the battery is charged."],
["electrolytes?","The liquid inside a battery that lets charged atoms travel between the two sides."],
["capacitors?","A tiny part that stores a little electricity and releases it to keep the power steady."],
["cataly(?:st|sts|ses|tic)","A substance that speeds up a chemical reaction without being used up itself."],
["isotopes?","A version of an element whose atoms hold a different number of neutrons, which makes them slightly heavier or lighter."],
["half-life","The time it takes for half of a radioactive substance to decay away."],
["radioactive","Giving off radiation as its unstable atoms break down, usually turning into other elements."],
["phosphors?","A material that glows when light or electricity hits it."],
["by-?products?","Something obtained on the side while producing something else."],
["brines?","Very salty water, pumped from underground or left in salt lakes."],
["ores?","Rock that holds enough of a metal to be worth mining."],
["smelt(?:ing|ed|er|ers)","Heating ore in a furnace with carbon or another substance that pulls the oxygen or sulphur away, leaving the metal. It is a chemical reaction, not just melting."],
["galvanis(?:ing|ed)","Coating steel with zinc so that it does not rust."],
["oxides?","A compound of an element with oxygen; rust is iron oxide."],
["reserves","The part of a resource that can be mined at a profit today."],
["artisanal","Mined by hand by individuals or small groups, not by a mining company."]
];

// ─── What the supply-risk sticker means ───
var RISK_EXPLAIN={
low:"Plenty of countries produce it and supply is rarely disrupted.",
medium:"Supply is fairly concentrated, or demand is rising quickly.",
high:"A few countries control most of the supply and it is hard to replace.",
critical:"The highest level: most of the supply comes from one country, or only as a by-product of another metal, and industry has no good substitute.",
special:"Not a mined commodity: it is made in reactors or laboratories, or exists only as traces."
};
var RISK_ABOUT="Supply risk is how easily the world's supply of an element could be disrupted. It rises when production is concentrated in a few countries, when the element only comes as a byproduct of something else, and when it is hard to recycle or replace. There are four levels: low, medium, high and critical. Critical is kept for the few elements whose supply hangs on one country or on a single by-product stream and which industry cannot easily replace. The official lists of the United States and the European Union simply say critical or not, and also weigh how important an element is to industry. The game's four levels are its own simplified reading of them.";

